---
source: crates/mesh-core/src/deploy.rs
expression: system.dropin_text()
---
# Fleet self-upgrade drop-in (M24 §4) — OPT IN, deliberately.
#
# Install with:
#
#   sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/meshnode.service.d
#   sudo cp meshnode-fleet-upgrade.conf \
#           /etc/systemd/system/meshnode.service.d/fleet-upgrade.conf
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart meshnode
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this changes, and why it is not the default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `meshnode upgrade --on <ring> --to <version>` asks each node to fetch and
# verify its own signed release and swap its own binary. **No binary is ever
# handed from node to node** — the mesh carries the decision, never the
# executable. But the node still has to be able to write the file, and the
# shipped unit deliberately makes that impossible: `ProtectSystem=full`
# renders /usr read-only for the service, and the daemon runs as the
# unprivileged `meshnode` user.
#
# That default is correct. A daemon that can rewrite its own binary is a
# daemon whose compromise is permanent, so granting it that power is a
# decision an operator makes on purpose, once, in a file they can see —
# never something a feature turns on for them.
#
# This drop-in makes exactly one directory writable: wherever meshnode is
# installed. Everything else about the hardening is untouched.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which path to use
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The line below assumes the tarball default, /usr/local/bin. Change it to
# match `which meshnode`.
#
# **Package installs (.deb/.rpm) must not use this.** A dpkg- or rpm-owned
# binary is upgraded through the package manager, and meshnode refuses to
# replace one itself — with a message naming apt or dnf. For those fleets the
# rollout tool is the one you already have; `meshnode upgrade --on` is for
# tarball installs.

[Service]
ReadWritePaths=/usr/local/bin
